(a) Primary responsibility for enforcement of this chapter shall be vested in the animal services director.
(b) The board of commissioners shall be authorized to create an animal services board which shall operate under the following bylaws:
(1) Created. An animal services advisory board is hereby created and established in Putnam County. This animal services advisory board shall serve in an advisory capacity to the board of county commissioners on matters relating to the welfare and control of the domesticated animal population within the county.
(2) Purpose. The purpose of the animal services advisory board is to provide information, advice and counsel to the board of county commissioners, county manager and the director of the animal services department with respect to the appropriate means and methods by which the county may adequately provide for the welfare of its domesticated animal population and for the protection of its citizens in relation to uncontrolled and/or dangerous dogs. The stated purpose of the advisory board shall not be construed as delegating to such animal services advisory board or any of its members any power or authority of a governmental, legislative or administrative nature, but merely is the establishment of an official instrument by which the board of county commissioners may receive the benefit of accurate, comprehensive information, advice and counsel concerning all matters relating to animal service programs which may be necessary for the appropriate action by the board of county commissioners.
(3) Duties and functions. The animal services advisory board shall have the duties, functions and responsibilities to:
a. Serve in an advisory capacity to the board of county commissioners, county manager and the director of the animal services department with respect to all matters pertaining to animal welfare and control policies and programs conducted within the county;
b. Carry out continuing study of animal services facilities and services in the county and report to the board of county commissioners recommended improvements in such services as needed by the county in respect to animal welfare and control;
c. Devise means and methods by which animal services facilities and services may be improved and more fully utilized to provide better service and availability to the general public;
d. Make a continuing study and periodic reports and recommendations for sound, feasible programs for financing the cost of improving animal services facilities and services;
e. Review all laws, ordinances, resolutions and regulations governing animal services programs and services and make recommendations concerning appropriate action that may be taken to improve same;
f. Participate in hearings and in the disposition of dangerous and potentially dangerous dogs as required by O.C.G.A. title 4, chapter 8, article 2;
g. Formulate plans and programs for the coordination of the activities of all the governmental entities and nongovernmental agencies, as well as corporations for profit or not for profit and individuals, relating to animal welfare and control programs, facilities and services within the county;
h. Formulate comprehensive, feasible plans or programs for providing an adequate animal welfare and control service to the unincorporated areas of the county and to those municipalities who have entered into any local agreements with the county to provide animal control services; and
i. Perform and carry out such other duties and functions of an advisory nature as may be assigned to the animal services advisory board by the board of county commissioners.
(4) Membership.
a. This board shall be composed of county citizens who are dedicated to the purpose of providing a sound animal welfare and control policy;
b. The animal services advisory board shall be composed of six members, with five voting members appointed by the board of county commissioners and one ex-officio nonvoting member. All members shall serve on a voluntary basis without compensation. The board members shall be selected according to the following:
1. One member shall be a member of a regional nonprofit animal welfare organization and shall be nominated by the chairperson of the board of county commissioners;
2. Four members shall be nominated from the citizens of the county; their nomination shall reflect their manifested interest in animal welfare and control programs conducted by the county. Each district commissioner shall have the opportunity to nominate one member as outlined in section 2-53;
3. The director of the animal services department shall serve as an ex-officio member of the board and as such will serve as a nonvoting member.
c. Each voting member shall be appointed for a four-year term;
d. The ex-officio nonvoting member of the advisory board shall serve consecutive terms without interruptions.
(5) Meetings.
a. The animal services advisory board shall meet at least every quarter. The meetings may be called by the chairperson or by written notice signed by three members of the said board in accordance with O.C.G.A. § 50-14-1(d);
b. Special meetings may be called in accordance with O.C.G.A. § 50-14-1(d) by the director of the animal services department in order to conduct hearings needed to make disposition of dangerous and potential dangerous dogs;
c. The presence of three voting members is required to constitute a quorum sufficient to the holding of a meeting or a taking of any action. A majority vote of those members present at any meeting shall be sufficient to enable the taking of any action;
d. The committee shall keep adequate records of its meetings, including the minutes, attendance records and records of formal committee action regarding animal services program review and monitoring;
e. The animal services advisory board shall select its chairperson and such other officers as may be deemed necessary or desirable and shall establish a time and procedure for the election of such officers on an annual basis;
f. Members shall attend meetings regularly in order to remain a member of the animal services advisory board in good standing. Absences shall be authorized by the chair of said board;
g. Additional procedures for the conduct of meetings, bylaws and regulations shall be developed by the animal services advisory board.
(6) Limitation on powers. The animal services advisory board shall have no power or authority to commit the county government to any policies or to incur any financial obligation or to create any liability on the part of the county. No actions or recommendations of the advisory committee shall be binding on the county until approved or adopted by the board of county commissioners.
(7) Amendments to bylaws. The provisions set forth herein may be amended by a majority vote of the voting members of the animal services advisory board. Any amendments shall be voted on at a regular meeting and all members shall receive a minimum of ten days' prior notice. The amendments approved by said board will require the concurrence of the Putnam County Board of Commissioners.
(Amend. of 3-18-2003; Amend. of 5-18-2010(1); Amend. of 3-1-2013; Amend. of 5-19-2015)
Editor's note— An amendment adopted May 19, 2015, changed the title of § 14-4 from "Animal control director; animal control board" to read as herein set out.

